   "en": {
    "headline": "Karpathy: the gap between what people think AI does and what it really does is growing",
    "summary_short": "Andrej Karpathy warns that the distance between how most people perceive AI and how those who use it intensively see it is widening. His post started one of the longest threads of the week on Hacker News, with more than 1,000 comments.",
    "summary_long": "As AI Explained summarises it, Karpathy describes several groups: about 6 billion people who do not use AI, between 1 and 2 billion who use “clunky” chatbots, about 0.2% (perhaps 20 million) who build apps and follow the news closely, and some 5,000 people at frontier labs, in a category of their own. That last group, and partly the one before it, feel “the vertigo of the curve going vertical”, while most people do not notice what is happening.\n\nThe original post on X is not among the downloaded sources, so this summary relies on AI Explained's account. The Hacker News thread drew 727 points and 1,072 comments.",
    "unconfirmed_claims": []
   },
